  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "K Bharathi vs The State Of Telangana And 2 Others on 15 December, 2020 Author: B. Vijaysen Reddy Bench: Raghvendra Singh Chauhan, B.Vijaysen Reddy High Court For The State Of Telangana The Hon'Ble The Chief Justice Raghvendra Singh Chauhan And The Hon'Ble Sri Justice B. Vijaysen Reddy Writ Petition No.15252 of 2020 Date: 15.12.2020 Between K. Bharathi. ... Petitioner And The State of Telangana, Rep. by its Principal Secretary, General Administration (Spl. (Law & Order) Department, Secretariat, Hyderabad and others. ...Respondents Counsel for the petitioner : Mr. Khaja Nizamuddin Counsel for the respondents : Mr. T. Srikanth Reddy Gp for Home",
    "facts": "Detenu moved bail of 2019 IPC in petition; APP opposed Dt.07.05.19 Cr.No.169 grant of bail and vide the detenu was released on bail vide release order dated 18.07.19. Detenu moved bail of 2019 IPC petition; APP opposed Dt.29.05.19 grant of bail and vide detenu was released on bail vide release order dated 18.07.19. 6. It is evident from the impugned detention order that the detaining authority has formed an opinion that the detenu may violate the bail conditions and there is imminent possibility of the detenu committing similar offences prejudicial to public order.",
    "arguments": "In a recent decision of this Court in RAPOLU MAHALAKSHMI v. STATE OF TELANGANA1 it was held as under: 12. Every violation of criminal law does cause a rippled in the society. But the ripples can be calmed down by use of normal Criminal Justice System. It is only when an offence strikes the society, like a tsunami, that as a weapon of the last resort, the detaining authority is justified in invoking the powers under preventive detention laws. The detaining authority should also examine whether the release of the detenu can be prevented by opposing his bail applications or not? Whether his detention under judicial custody can be ensured or not? In PUSHKAR MUKHERJEE v. STATE OF WEST BENGAL2, the Supreme Court held as under: (1969) 1 SCC 10 K Bharathi 5 connection with the maintenance of public order.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In a recent decision of this Court in RAPOLU MAHALAKSHMI v. STATE OF TELANGANA1 it was held as under: 12. Every violation of criminal law does cause a rippled in the society. But the ripples can be calmed down by use of normal Criminal Justice System. It is only when an offence strikes the society, like a tsunami, that as a weapon of the last resort, the detaining authority is justified in invoking the powers under preventive detention laws. The detaining authority should also examine whether the release of the detenu can be prevented by opposing his bail applications or not? Whether his detention under judicial custody can be ensured or not? In PUSHKAR MUKHERJEE v. STATE OF WEST BENGAL2, the Supreme Court held as under: (1969) 1 SCC 10 K Bharathi 5 connection with the maintenance of public order."
  },
